Set a flight log against a simulation of it: how far the logged height and speed lie from the simulated, and each reading beside the simulation's

Usage: hpr compare [OPTIONS] <LOG> <DESIGN>

Arguments:
  <LOG>
          The flight log: a file in any format HPR Sim reads, or another logger's file read by --mapping or --layout

  <DESIGN>
          The design: an OpenRocket .ork file, an HPR design (.hpr, or .hprz with its attachments), or a rocket described in JSON (.json)

Options:
      --config <NAME>
          The motor configuration to fly: its number in the list the output shows, its name, or its id. Without it, the design's default configuration flies, or its only one

      --json
          Print one JSON document on standard output instead of text, errors included

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use color. With auto, output to a terminal is colored, and NO_COLOR, FORCE_COLOR and CLICOLOR_FORCE are followed. JSON output never has color
          
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, always, never]

      --motor <NAME_OR_FILE>
          Fly this motor in place of the configuration's, ignited at launch: a catalog designation or common name, or a .eng or .rse file

      --mount <NAME>
          The motor mount that --motor goes in, by its name or its component id; it needs --motor. Without it, the motor goes in the configuration's mount, or the design's only one

      --delay <S_OR_P>
          The ejection delay of the motor --motor names: seconds from burnout to the charge (0 fires it at burnout), or P for a plugged motor. It needs --motor. Without it, the motor fires no charge

      --latitude <DEG>
          The launch site's latitude, degrees north (south is negative)
          
          [default: 0]

      --longitude <DEG>
          The launch site's longitude, degrees east (west is negative)
          
          [default: 0]

      --elevation <M>
          The launch site's height above sea level, m
          
          [default: 0]

      --rail-length <M>
          The rail's length, m, from the rocket's aft end to the rail's top
          
          [default: 1.5]

      --inclination <DEG>
          The rail's angle above the horizon, degrees [default: 90, vertical]

      --heading <DEG>
          The direction the rail leans toward, degrees clockwise from true north [default: 0]

      --wind <M_S>
          A wind of this speed at every height, m/s [default: calm]

      --wind-from <DEG>
          The direction the wind blows from, degrees clockwise from true north
          
          [default: 0]

      --weather <FILE>
          Fly the air and winds of a weather profile, the JSON file `hpr weather` writes, in place of the standard atmosphere and --wind. The site's --elevation must lie within the profile's heights, and its levels must give winds

      --accept-design-errors
          Fly a design whose checks find errors, such as a motor wider than its mount. The flight may not be physical

      --offline
          Look up a motor the bundled catalog lacks in the cache of ThrustCurve.org files only, never on the network

      --mapping <FILE>
          Read the log as a table, by this column mapping: a JSON file, as `hpr analyze --print-mapping` prints one (schema/cli/analyze-mapping.schema.json)

      --layout <FILE>
          Read the log as binary records, or a serial console's capture of them, by this record layout: a JSON file (schema/cli/analyze-layout.schema.json)

      --sheet <N>
          Which sheet of a workbook (.xlsx or .xls) to read, counting from 0

      --height-reads <WHAT>
          What the log's height stands for: a barometer's reading, or a height climbed. Without it, a log's main altitude, or a column you mapped, is taken as a barometer's

          Possible values:
          - barometer:      A barometer's reading: the standard atmosphere's altitude of the pressure, less the pad's
          - height-climbed: A height climbed: a GPS receiver's, or an accelerometer's integral

      --recording <N>
          Which recording to read from a file that holds several, counting from 0

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Examples:
  Set a log against a simulation of it:
      hpr compare flight.pf2 rocket.ork --motor CTI-J350
  The same, in the launch day's wind:
      hpr compare flight.pf2 rocket.ork --motor CTI-J350 --wind 4

Guide: https://hpr.fusionspace.co/cli.html#hpr-compare
